            {
              "id": "eaf8bacd",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "text-editor",
              "settings": {
                "editor": "\n<p>The <strong>Badri cow</strong> is an indigenous hill breed found only in the Uttarakhand Himalayas — named after the sacred Badrinath region.</p>\n<p>What makes Badri ghee rare:</p>\n<ul style=\"line-height:2;padding-left:20px;font-family:Inter,sans-serif;\">\n<li><strong>The breed:</strong> One of 50 officially recognised indigenous cattle breeds of India (NBAGR registered). Not a cross-breed.</li>\n<li><strong>The diet:</strong> Natural high-altitude grazing — alpine herbs, clean Himalayan water, no feedlot, no hormones.</li>\n<li><strong>The milk yield:</strong> Badri cows give only ~2–3 litres/day vs. 15–20 for commercial breeds. This is why Badri ghee is rare.</li>\n<li><strong>The process:</strong> Same bilona method — hand-churned, slow-cooked, zero shortcuts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>[ADD: specific location / herd / cooperative details here.]</p>\n",
                "typography_typography": "custom",
                "typography_font_family": "Inter",
                "typography_font_size": {
                  "unit": "px",
                  "size": 16,
                  "sizes": []
                },
                "typography_line_height": {
                  "unit": "em",
                  "size": 1.85,
                  "sizes": []
                }
              }
